# Human review with Crit

## When to use Crit

Use Crit before implementation when materially different product, interface, architecture, compatibility, security, or data choices remain and user feedback could change the direction. First write a concise design note; do not send an unresolved prompt to review.

Use Crit before pushing nontrivial code that changes observable behavior, interfaces, architecture, data handling, or security. Skip trivial mechanical changes and docs-only changes unless the user asks. Explicit Crit requests always qualify.

Do not push while Crit comments remain unresolved unless the user explicitly directs it. Agent-only feedback belongs in OMP `/review`, not Crit.

Choose arguments from the request and current context; do not ask the user to select a Crit mode:

```bash
crit <file-or-directory>
crit --pr <number-or-url>
crit --range <base>..<head>
crit
```

Run Crit in the foreground and wait for the human to finish. Relay the printed local review URL so the user can comment in the browser. Do not read comments early or claim approval before Crit exits.

After it exits, inspect its JSON result. If `approved` is true, report that no changes were requested. If false, address each unresolved comment, then run the exact `Next round: crit ...` command printed by Crit. Continue until the user finishes with no unresolved feedback or explicitly ends the loop.

Use `crit comments --json` only when re-entering a review without its finish output. Respect comment quotes and anchors over stale line numbers. Do not resolve comments unless the user asks.

For a share URL, use `crit share <file>` and relay the complete output. Do not share or publish unless requested.
